If a finite set of tree tokens entails a token, inserting that token preserves consistency.

Induct on the token. For the bottom, insert it into the consistent set given by the entailment. For an atom, the nonempty atom projection forces both pair projections to be empty, and the inserted set is left-consistent: the atom projection gains that atom and stays consistent by the entailment-consistency axiom, while inserting an atom leaves both pair projections empty. For a left-pair token, the inserted set is right-consistent: inserting it leaves the atom projection empty and leaves the right pair projection unchanged and consistent, and the left pair projection gains the payload. The payload disjunction either gives an entailment of that payload by the left pair projection, to which the inductive hypothesis applies, or says that the payload is the bottom, in which case the bottom is inserted into the consistent left pair projection. The right-pair case is symmetric.
